# TODO

1. A force switch, so that an existing CHANGELOG can be overwritten rather than the directory being skipped.
2. Take the binary's name from the CHANGELOG, rather than assuming the sole entry in bin/.
3. Produce a single CHANGELOG in the last version directory, rather than a cumulative one in every directory.
4. Work for git repositories, whether by writing outside the repository or by rewriting history.
5. A tier for a snapshot: a revision whose header carries no new version and no new Changes item. Such a revision is a working copy taken between versions, not a version, and today its entry is summarised by the first line of its diff, so a revision in which a fixture was regenerated under the next name and the test script changed reads as `- test/6.0.csv`. SimpleCSV's staging tree has seven (`0.2.2` once, `0.4.6` four times, `0.4.9` once, and a second `0.9.3`). Summarise such a revision by the whole diff, and mark it in the subject as a snapshot rather than a release; the form of that mark is undecided.
6. Take the entry's date from the latest header in the tree, not only the main file's. SimpleCSV's second `0.9.3` changed only `String/split_csv.rb`, whose header says 20100612, and was dated 20100526 from the library header. When these two are done, the imported histories can have their narration regenerated in one pass: `dsv` needs a re-import through `_github_staging/SimpleCSV/synthetic/` and a force-push, so it is done once, before any consumer points at the repository.
